💭 that it does not have, and let go, in a loop.
💭
💭 Expected: memory stays as it is.
💭 What happens: the dictionary's one entry is never freed. The private 🔍 of 🍯 walks a
💭 bucket's chain and returns its loop's variable from inside the loop (s/🍯.🍇), which
💭 is 24-returning-a-local-early.🍇 in the library itself: an entry that the walk goes
💭 past, because its key is another, is retained and never released. So a dictionary
💭 leaks whenever a key is looked up or set in a bucket that holds another. Which keys
💭 share a bucket changes from run to run, since the hash of a text is seeded at random
💭 when the process starts (runtime::internal::seed): with eight keys set and none looked
💭 up, 100,000 dictionaries left between 23 MB and 163 MB behind in twenty runs. Twenty
💭 lookups of absent keys make it all but certain that the one entry is walked past.
